static const struct KakuNote U3N[] = {
	{ "Three verb groups", "Every verb falls into one of three groups, and the group decides how it conjugates. る-verbs end in -iru or -eru and simply swap る for ます (たべる → たべます). う-verbs change the final u-sound to an i-sound before ます (のむ → のみます). There are only two irregulars: する → します and くる → きます.", U3N0EX, 2 },
	{ "ます and ません", "ます is the polite present. It covers both habits and the future, so いきます is \"I go\" and \"I will go\"; context decides. The negative is ません.", U3N1EX, 2 },
	{ "を, で, に, へ", "を marks the direct object, the thing the verb acts on. で marks where an action happens. に marks a destination or a specific time, and also the place you arrive at. へ can replace に for destinations.", U3N2EX, 2 },
	{ "Which time words take に", "Times you can point to on a clock or calendar take に: さんじに, にちようびに. Relative words — きょう, あした, まいにち, こんしゅう — never take に. Neither does いつ.", U3N3EX, 2 },
	{ "〜ませんか", "The negative question is how you invite someone, like \"won't you...?\" in English. It is softer than ましょう, which assumes agreement.", U3N4EX, 2 },
	{ "Frequency adverbs", "Put them before the verb: まいにち (every day), よく (often), ときどき (sometimes), あまり (not much), ぜんぜん (not at all). The last two demand a negative verb — あまり たべません.", U3N5EX, 2 },
	{ "Word order is flexible, the verb is not", "Because particles label each role, you can shuffle the phrases around for emphasis. The one rule that never bends: the verb goes last.", U3N6EX, 2 },
};
