static const struct KakuNote U5N[] = {
	{ "Two kinds of adjective", "い-adjectives end in い and carry their own tense: たかい, たかくないです. な-adjectives behave like nouns and lean on です: しずかです, しずかじゃないです. The difference matters in every pattern that follows, so learn which kind each new word is.", U5N0EX, 2 },
	{ "Adjectives in the past", "い-adjectives change their own ending: たかかったです, たかくなかったです. な-adjectives just change です to でした or じゃなかったです. いい is irregular throughout — よかったです, not いかったです.", U5N1EX, 2 },
	{ "Adjectives in front of nouns", "Put the adjective straight before the noun. い-adjectives attach directly (おおきい いぬ), な-adjectives need their な (しずかな まち). That な is the whole reason for the name.", U5N2EX, 2 },
	{ "好き and きらい", "Both are な-adjectives, not verbs, so the thing liked is marked with が, not を. Stack だい in front for \"really like\" and あまり with a negative for \"not much\".", U5N3EX, 2 },
	{ "〜ましょう and 〜ましょうか", "Swap ます for ましょう to say \"let's\". Adding か makes it a question — \"shall we?\" — which leaves the other person room to say no.", U5N4EX, 2 },
	{ "Counting things with 〜つ", "The ひとつ ふたつ みっつ set counts objects up to ten with no counter word needed. It is the safe fallback when you cannot remember the specific counter.", U5N5EX, 2 },
};
