static const struct KakuNote U6N[] = {
	{ "The te-form", "The single most useful form in the language, and the one worth drilling until it is automatic. る-verbs: drop る, add て (たべる → たべて). う-verbs follow their final sound: う/つ/る → って, む/ぶ/ぬ → んで, く → いて, ぐ → いで, す → して. いく is the odd one out: いって.", U6N0EX, 2 },
	{ "〜てください", "te-form plus ください makes a polite request. Softer than an order, firmer than a hint. Leaving off ください makes it casual, for people close to you.", U6N1EX, 2 },
	{ "Linking actions with the te-form", "The te-form also strings actions together in sequence: do this, then that. Only the final verb carries the tense, so everything before it stays in te-form however far in the past the whole sentence is.", U6N2EX, 2 },
	{ "〜てもいいです", "Permission: \"it is fine to...\". As a question it asks for permission, which is how you will mostly use it.", U6N3EX, 2 },
	{ "〜てはいけません", "The flat prohibition — \"you must not\". Strong, so it comes from someone with authority: a teacher, a sign, a rule. In speech it often contracts to 〜ちゃだめ.", U6N4EX, 2 },
	{ "〜から for reasons", "から means \"because\" and goes at the end of the reason, not the start. The order is reason から result, the reverse of English.", U6N5EX, 2 },
	{ "〜ましょうか to offer help", "The same ましょうか from Lesson 5 doubles as an offer when you are the only one doing the work: \"shall I...?\".", U6N6EX, 2 },
};
