static const struct KakuNote U8N[] = {
	{ "Short forms", "The dictionary form is the short present: たべる, のむ, いく. The negative drops ます and uses ない: たべない, のまない, いかない. For う-verbs the final sound becomes あ, except う-endings which become わ — かう → かわない. する → しない, くる → こない, ある → ない.", U8N0EX, 2 },
	{ "When to use short forms", "Short forms are how friends and family talk. Use them down the social ladder or sideways to people you know well, and keep ます for teachers, strangers and anyone at work. Getting this wrong sounds rude rather than friendly.", U8N1EX, 2 },
	{ "〜と思います", "To say what you think, put the thought in short form, then と思います. The と is the quotation marker — everything before it is the thought.", U8N2EX, 2 },
	{ "〜と言っていました", "The same structure reports what someone said. Mark the speaker with は, quote in short form, finish with といっていました.", U8N3EX, 2 },
	{ "〜ないでください", "The negative request: please do not. Take the short negative, drop the い, add でください.", U8N4EX, 2 },
	{ "〜のが好きです", "To like doing something, turn the verb into a noun with の, then mark it with が. The same trick works with じょうず (good at) and へた (bad at).", U8N5EX, 2 },
	{ "なにか and なにも", "なにか means \"something\" and takes no particle. なにも means \"nothing\" and needs a negative verb. The same split runs through だれか／だれも and どこか／どこも.", U8N6EX, 2 },
};
