static const char* const C0B[] = { "In English, \"the dog bit the man\" and \"the man bit the dog\" use exactly the same words. The only thing telling you who bit whom is the order. Move a word and you change the meaning.", "Japanese does not work that way. Each word carries a small tag — a particle — saying what job it is doing in the sentence. Because the job is written on the word itself, the order can move and the meaning does not. That is why a Japanese sentence can look scrambled to an English eye and still be perfectly clear.", "So a particle is not decoration and it is not a preposition. It is a label saying: this word is the topic, this one is the thing being acted on, this one is where it happens. Learn the job each particle marks and the sentences stop being a word jumble.", "One more thing worth knowing early: the verb goes at the end, always. Everything before it is labelled pieces in more or less any order." };
static const char* const C0S[] = { "は", "the topic — what the sentence is about", "を", "the thing the verb acts on", "で", "where the action happens", "に", "where you are heading, or a point in time", "の", "glues two nouns together", "も", "too, as well — it replaces は", "か", "turns the sentence into a question" };
static const char* const C0E[] = { "わたしはにくをたべます。", "I eat meat.", "わたし is the topic, にく is the thing being eaten. Two different jobs, two different tags.", "うちでたべます。", "I eat at home.", "で because the eating happens there.", "うちにかえります。", "I go home.", "に because home is where you end up. Same word, different job, different particle.", "わたしのほん", "my book", "の glues them: the second noun is the main thing, the first describes it.", "そらさんもがくせいです。", "Sora is a student too.", "も replaces は. You never write もは." };
static const char* const C0L[] = { "がっこうでいきます。", "がっこうにいきます。", "いく needs a destination, not a venue. You are not doing something at the school, you are heading to it.", "うちにたべます。", "うちでたべます。", "Eating is an action that happens somewhere, so で. に would mean you are travelling into the house.", "わたしもはがくせいです。", "わたしもがくせいです。", "も takes the place of は. The two never sit together.", "にくがたべます。", "にくをたべます。", "The meat is being eaten, so it is the object: を." };
