static const char* const C12B[] = { "Up to ten you simply learn them. After that they stack: 23 is にじゅうさん, literally two-ten-three. 45 is よんじゅうご. Hundreds and thousands work the same way.", "Four and seven have two readings each — よん/し and なな/しち — and which one you use depends on what follows. For counting and for most counters, よん and なな are the safe ones.", "The part that catches people out is the hundreds, where three numbers change sound: 300 is さんびゃく not さんひゃく, 600 is ろっぴゃく, and 800 is はっぴゃく. Those three are worth saying out loud until they are automatic, because they turn up in prices constantly." };
